<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Henry Dunant</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">1828–1910</span> · Also known as Jean-Henri Dunant</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Henry Dunant was a Swiss businessman and humanitarian from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/geneva">Geneva</a>. Shocked by the suffering of wounded soldiers after the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-solferino">Battle of Solferino</a> in 1859, he wrote A Memory of Solferino, which called for neutral relief societies and an international agreement to protect the wounded. This led to the founding of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/international-committee-of-the-red-cross">International Committee of the Red Cross</a> in 1863 and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/first-geneva-convention">Geneva Convention of 1864</a>, and he shared the first <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nobel-peace-prize">Nobel Peace Prize</a> in 1901.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/henry-dunant.jpg" alt="Henry Dunant" width="320" height="404" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Frédéric Boissonnas · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Henry_Dunant-young.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
